content.text:
I found four bugs in the resolver and one in the semver helpers, and fixed all five. `node --test` passes (1/1), and a throwaway script covering the cases below also passed before I deleted it.

**Why CI picked older versions** (`src/resolver.js`): `candidateOrder` compared versions as joined text, so `1.9.0` ranked above `1.10.0`. It now compares the three numbers, highest first.

**Why CI said "no solution" for solvable sets** (`src/resolver.js`):
- **Backtracking didn't undo anything.** `restore` only removed the selected version. Constraints and queued names added by the abandoned choice stayed active and could wrongly rule out other versions. It now trims the queue and constraint list back to where they were when that choice was made.
- **Already-selected packages added their dependencies again.** The contract says they shouldn't; doing so repeated constraints and queued names over and over. Now they're skipped, because their constraints are already checked when they're discovered.
- **There was a 4096-step cap.** It returned `null` on any long dependency chain. I removed it. The search still always finishes: each choice picks a new package and each package has a limited list of versions. It uses a loop, not recursion, so chain length doesn't hit the call-stack limit.

**Semver** (`src/semver.js`): `^` always allowed up to the next major version. Following the contract, a zero major now limits it to the next minor (`^0.2.3` stops before `0.3.0`), and `0.0.x` limits it to the next patch.

The first-found selection order, the `resolve(registry, root)` export and the semver API are unchanged.

The throwaway script checked:
- backtracking discards constraints and dependencies from abandoned choices;
- cycles and self-dependencies, including one that can't be satisfied;
- a 50,000-package chain;
- a missing package returns `null`;
- invalid entries throw `TypeError` even when unreachable;
- an empty root returns `{}`;
- the caret boundaries and `compare('1.10.0', '1.9.0')`.

I didn't change `test/`, so the repo still only tests the version-ordering case.
